{align:justify|In a village in southern Thailand, a young man surnamed Luu celebrated his wedding, and the bride was a girl nicknamed "Miss Monkey."}
{align:justify|At first everyone assumed "Miss Monkey" must be an extremely ugly girl, or a maiden covered in hair and shaped just like a monkey — hence the nickname.}
{align:justify|But when the newlyweds finished bowing to Heaven and Earth and came out arm in arm to greet the guests, everyone's eyes lit up, and all cried out as one from the bottom of their hearts:}
{align:justify|"Heavens! The bride is beautiful as a fairy!..."}
{align:justify|Yet all were burning with curiosity about the name "Miss Monkey" and longed to inquire into her story.}
{align:justify|It turned out that "Miss Monkey's" mother had in her youth been a celebrated beauty, with countless young men at her feet. Admirers from all around came hoping for one glimpse of her face; many earnestly sought her hand. Even a rich young man from neighboring Malaysia crossed the border to propose. But the beauty gracefully declined, saying she did not wish to marry a foreigner and leave her parents.}
{align:justify|Before long the beauty married her childhood sweetheart from the same village. He was handsome and gifted, she was radiant and lovely — truly a match made in Heaven. All the young men and women around envied them.}
{align:justify|Soon the bride was with child, and her husband cared for her more tenderly than ever, providing for her every need. Her parents-in-law were all delighted and showered her with kindness.}
{align:justify|Normally she ate easily, but with pregnancy it was the reverse: however delicious the food, she could not swallow a bite.}
{align:justify|Her husband racked his brains a hundred ways to tempt his beloved wife's appetite.}
{align:justify|One day he bought a monkey's foot at market, stewed it low with northern medicinal herbs like yam, ginseng, angelica, and the like, then set it before her by surprise — and at last aroused her craving. Seeing his wife eat heartily, he was overjoyed.}
{align:justify|From then on, every market day he bought home a monkey's foot or a piece of monkey meat for his wife.}
{align:justify|One day at market he met a hunter selling a bound monkey. Delighted, the husband bought it and brought it home, thinking to keep it a few days before killing it for his wife to eat.}
{align:justify|That day his wife was seven months pregnant, sitting in an armchair watching her husband kill the monkey.}
{align:justify|The pitiful monkey had for days been fed nothing but bananas and fruit; the fat on its body amounted to less than half a pound, the rest to serve as tonic for the expectant mother.}
{align:justify|Seeing the husband take up a knife to kill it, the monkey's eyes streamed with tears and it knelt on its legs like a human, begging its master to spare its life.}
{align:justify|But this wife-doting husband felt not a stir, not a spark of mercy. Raising the blade, he aimed at its heart.}
{align:justify|At the brink of death, the monkey swiftly seized the handle with its right hand and gripped the blade tight with its left, resisting death with all its strength, refusing to let go. The husband strained but could not drive the knife in. Thinking to draw the knife back and stab again, in that very motion of withdrawal — because the monkey's four fingers gripped so tightly — two segments of its fingers were sliced clean off, leaving only the last joints of the four fingers, counting inward from the knuckle. The expectant mother saw blood gushing like a stream; her cruel resolve melted, and she cried out again and again for her husband to stop: "Don't kill it! Kill no more! Set the monkey free at once!"}
{align:justify|Through relentless struggle the monkey snatched its life back from the gates of hell. Enduring the pain, it bolted for the door — and before leaving the gate it turned back to look at the couple, its eyes full of hatred, then vanished without a trace.}
{align:justify|From then on, the wife vomited at the smell of meat and ate only vegetables and tofu. Her family, fearing she lacked nourishment, secretly mixed meat broth into her food — but one taste made her retch violently.}
{align:justify|When her time came, she bore a baby girl whose features were perfectly formed — except that on her left hand there was only a thumb and the last joints of four fingers, shaped exactly as if sliced by a sharp blade. Parents and relatives sighed at the sight, murmuring, "How strange!"}
{align:justify|Thailand is Buddhist and believes deeply in cause and effect; all said as one: "This is karmic retribution, come from the husband's severing of the monkey's four fingers."}
{align:justify|Today the bride "Miss Monkey" wears a white wedding gown and white gloves, gentle and lovely as a fairy descended to earth.}
{align:justify|Yet who would know that inside those gloves there are no four long slender fingers — only four remaining stumps, as if chopped off? That is the origin of the nickname "Miss Monkey," a story on everyone's lips in those days.}
